Texture quads: a caller-owned texture on a perspective face.

`paint_image_quad` (b6) puts an atlas image on a keystoned quad. This adds
`Window::paint_texture_quad`, the same face sampling a `UserTextureId`
instead, so a frame that lives in a dynamic texture (c6) can ride a quad
with real perspective. Covers blade (Linux, and macOS under `macos-blade`)
and DirectX 11.

A polychrome batch binds one atlas page and a user texture isn't in the
atlas, so this is a primitive kind of its own, `TextureSprite`, batched on
its texture id and slotted into `BatchIterator`'s merge right after the
polychrome sprites. Its GPU struct is the polychrome sprite's face without
the atlas parts: no tile, no grayscale, no corner radii, and the crop is a
0..1 `source` rather than a shrunken tile. The primitive's bounds are the
corners' bounding box and the homography comes from `quad_homography`, both
through `quad_face`, the steps `paint_image_quad` takes inline. A quad
leaning past its own horizon is skipped, the b6 rule, and so is an empty
crop, which has no texel centers to clamp the sample to.

Color: the polychrome fragment works on sRGB-encoded, straight-alpha
values, since the atlas and the surface are plain Unorm (blade surfaces
are configured `ColorSpace::Srgb`, DirectX targets B8G8R8A8_UNORM). User
textures are Rgba8UnormSrgb with straight alpha, so their samples arrive
linear. The new fragment stages encode the sample back with the exact sRGB
curve before the wash, fade and blend, which then match the polychrome
path. HLSL's own `linear_to_srgb` is a 2.2 power that decodes rather than
encodes, so the DirectX stage carries its own.

A released id paints nothing. Release drops the id from the renderer's map
there and then, so a quad painted in the frame of its release finds nothing
at draw time and the batch is skipped; blade's retirement list (c6) keeps
the texture alive for a frame still in flight, and D3D11 does that on its
own.

blade: a `tex-sprites` pipeline beside `poly-sprites`, with `vs_tex_sprite`
and `fs_tex_sprite` in shaders.wgsl reusing `PolySpriteVarying`, and the
mask capture replays these faces the way it replays images. DirectX: a
`texture_sprite` module in shaders.hlsl, compiled by build.rs with the
built-ins, bound with the texture's view from the user shader texture map.
The Metal renderer is untouched, and without `macos-blade` its batch match
lacks the new arm; rox never builds it.

Not a backport. A gpui bump carries this forward with b6 and c6.

